          "John also gives two short definitions: “God is love” (1 John 4:8, 16) and “God is light, and in him is no darkness at all” (1 John 1:5). Paul’s description of love in 1 Corinthians 13 then reads like a portrait of God’s own heart: love “is patient and is kind… doesn’t seek its own way… takes no account of evil” (1 Cor 13:4-5). Philippians 2:5-8 shows that the Son did not grab at status but humbled himself. And Hebrews 13:8 and James 1:17 tell us this character does not shift: there is “no variation, nor turning shadow.”",
          "Early Christians said the same. The second-century <em>Letter to Diognetus</em> says God sent his Son “as seeking to persuade, not to compel us; for violence has no place in the character of God.” Irenaeus taught that God’s greatness is beyond us, but “as regards His love, He is always known” through the Word."
